---

Mohnish Vats started out building websites and apps for clients in Patna, India. Most of those clients turned out to be the same kind of person: a YouTuber or a coaching teacher who had built an audience and wanted to sell courses to it. Around 80% of his first 30 clients were educators.

Every one of them wanted the same thing. A branded app where students could watch live classes, take tests, download notes, and pay fees. So rather than build it from scratch each time, Mohnish and his brothers built it once, and named the company [Creators Mind](https://www.creatorsmind.co.in/).

Three years later, that one app runs as more than 1,100 separate apps, each carrying a different coaching institute's name, and the students using them add up to almost 10 million people. **Every one of those apps runs on Appwrite Cloud.**

# The challenge

Indian test preparation is a large, fragmented market. A single educator with a strong YouTube following can have hundreds of thousands of students, but most of them do not have an engineering team. They have a phone, a whiteboard, and a Telegram group.

Creators Mind wanted to sell these educators a complete app under their own brand, with the same feature set every time and light customization where needed. That created two engineering problems.

**The first was cloning.** Each new client meant a new copy of the app, the backend, the data model, and every server-side function. If that took days of manual setup per client, the business could not scale.

**The second was cost.** Creators Mind charges educators a one-time setup fee plus a share of their course sales, and covers the servers and support out of that share. Backend bills that grow per client, or per database read, come straight off the margin. The team needed infrastructure they could predict.

# What is Creators Mind?

Creators Mind describes itself as a one-stop digital partner for teachers, coaching institutes, and schools. They offer a learning management system delivered as a branded Android app and website for each educator. Inside it, educators can:

- Run **live classes** and publish recorded videos
- Build **test series** and quizzes
- Share **PDF notes** and study material
- **Collect fees** and run their own store
- **Answer student doubts** and host a community

![Two Creators Mind coaching apps, each carrying the educator's own branding](/images/blog/customer-story-creators-mind/prototype.avif)

The hardware side sells digital boards and flat panels for classrooms, and the company also sets up teaching studios and handles digital marketing for educators. The team is 105 people. Last financial year, Creators Mind reported revenue of about $1.1 million (₹9.5 crore), with hardware making up about 70% and software the rest.

Educators running on the platform include **Law Prep**, **Aakash**, **Mentors Eduserve**, **Disha Online Classes**, **Target Board**, **Ask Class**, and **Ask Board**. Several of them have YouTube channels with over a million subscribers.

# Building Creators Mind on Appwrite

Before Appwrite, the learning management system (LMS) ran on Firebase. It did not survive the cloning model.

> "There were so many problems when I tried to clone the same app again and again. And the database read costs. When we saw Appwrite, the best thing was that you can clone the same app. If I build the same thing, I can clone it. That is the feature that made us use Appwrite."

Creators Mind started on self-hosted Appwrite, then moved to Appwrite Cloud while it was still in beta. Mohnish says he sent plenty of feedback on Discord in those early days, and talked to Appwrite's founder, Eldad, more than once about missing pieces. He stayed through thick and thin, and the platform grew up alongside his product. Today the company is growing into one of Appwrite's biggest Pro plan organizations from India.

The base project is the product. Onboarding an educator means cloning it and applying their branding. Under the hood, each app uses:

- **[Appwrite TablesDB](/docs/products/databases/tablesdb)** for courses, tests, students, orders, and everything else the app stores
- **[Appwrite Functions](/docs/products/functions)** for server-side logic like payments and content processing
- **[Appwrite Messaging](/docs/products/messaging)** for notifications to students
- **[Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites)** for hosting educator websites, with around 50 sites deployed so far

About 90% of Creators Mind's educators run on this shared Appwrite Cloud setup. The remaining 10% ask for their own server, and Creators Mind deploys the same app there with a maintenance fee instead of a revenue share. Because Appwrite is open source and can be [self-hosted](/docs/advanced/self-hosting), the app, data model, and functions move to the educator's infrastructure unchanged.

Mohnish also runs a second company, Super Squad, which builds apps, websites, and AI automation for clients outside education. Clients there often arrive asking for Supabase because a friend recommended it.

> "I convince many people to use Appwrite, because there are many things that are better than Supabase."

# Creators Mind today

The company that started as three brothers now has 105 people, an organic following of tens of thousands of educators and teachers, and, by Mohnish's account, the top-selling classroom hardware business in Bihar, India. Where the business stands today:

- **More than 1,100 educator apps live** on the platform
- Almost **10 million students** reached
- **About $1.1 million (₹9.5 crore) in revenue last financial year**, about 30% of it from software
- **Three years on Appwrite**, from the Cloud beta to today

None of it is paid acquisition. Educators find Creators Mind through other educators.

> "Appwrite gives us the backend power to build, ship, and scale without the backend overhead."

What's next for Creators Mind is more of the same, plus a look at [Appwrite's managed PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql) for the parts of the Super Squad client work that currently land on Supabase. Pro plan projects already include $10 of database compute credit per month, enough to cover a Starter database, so the team can try it without a new bill.

Creators Mind shows what a white-label product looks like when the backend is designed to be cloned. One project, one data model, one set of functions, and over a thousand educators each running what feels like their own app. Learn more about Creators Mind on their [website](https://www.creatorsmind.co.in/) and [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/creators-mind/).
